WHAT WAS CREATED

  dl/lib_moxa_rust_ies_auto_mibs/{Cargo.toml,Makefile,src/lib.rs}
  dl/lib_moxa_rust_snmp_agentx/{Cargo.toml,Makefile,src/lib.rs}
  moxa/package/net/lib_moxa_rust_{ies_auto_mibs,snmp_agentx}/{Config.in,*.mk}
  + the source line, OVERRIDE_SRCDIR and defconfig entry each package needs

  Both follow dl/lib_moxa_rust_system: source-only packages whose Buildroot
  recipe runs clippy and nothing else, linking through `#[link(name = "...")]`
  on the extern block rather than a build.rs. No sibling here has a build.rs.

  THAT MEANS `make <crate>` PROVES ALMOST NOTHING. The package build does not
  compile the crate; the real compile happens when app_moxa_framework picks it
  up as a path dependency in Task 5. So both crates were also built and linted
  directly against the target:

    cargo build  --release --target=armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf   both OK
    cargo clippy --release --target=armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf   0 warnings

  That matters beyond ceremony: c_ulong is 32-bit on the target and 64-bit on
  the host, and the OID conversions sit exactly on that seam.

TESTS — lib_moxa_rust_ies_auto_mibs                               5/5 PASS

    test_lookup_device_io            ok
    test_lookup_is_longest_prefix    ok
    test_known_flag_is_set           ok
    test_unknown_oid                 ok
    test_entry_count_is_not_empty    ok

  Run against a host build of the C library:

    gcc -fPIC -c iesmap.c <snmpd's -D set> ; ar rcs libmoxa_ies_auto_mibs.a
    RUSTFLAGS="-L native=<dir>" cargo test

  test_known_flag_is_set is the one that earns its place. Rust re-declares the
  flag numbers as `enum Flag`, and nothing in the type system ties them to
  IESMAP_FLAG_* in the C header. Asserting the constant would only restate the
  Rust side, so the test looks up portMirror/enable and asserts
  ReplaceTruthValue is set and UriStatus is not - which fails if either the
  numbering or the bit layout drifts. The C side is already tied to
  ies-auto-mibs by compile-time assertions (Task 3).

A REAL BUG THE RUST TESTS FOUND IN TASK 3's C CODE

  iesmap_load_once() set its "loaded" flag BEFORE building the table:

      if (g_loaded) return;
      g_loaded = 1;
      ies_auto_mibs_load_mibs();

  A second thread arriving mid-build saw "loaded", skipped the wait, and
  scanned a table that was still filling - so lookups returned no match while
  iesmap_entry_count() looked healthy. Fixed with pthread_once. After
  initialisation the table is read-only, so lookups still take no lock.

  cargo runs tests on parallel threads by default, which is why this surfaced
  here and not in Task 3's single-threaded C test. The library is advertised
  for "app_moxa_framework and other daemons", so this was a real defect and not
  a test artefact.

  DIAGNOSIS NOTE, because it cost time and would cost it again: after
  rebuilding the .a, cargo did NOT relink the test binary - it does not track
  an external library it was only told about through RUSTFLAGS. Three
  consecutive runs at 2, 4 and 8 threads gave identical results and looked like
  a deterministic non-race, which is what a stale binary looks like. Touch a
  source file (or cargo clean) after rebuilding the C side.

ONE ADDITION TO THE TASK 3 C API

    size_t iesmap_flag_bytes(void);

  So the binding can copy the flag array without hardcoding IESMAP_FLAG_BYTES
  and drifting from it. Three lines, and it removes a duplicated constant
  permanently. NEEDED is unchanged (libnetsnmp, libmoxa_util, libgcc_s, libc);
  pthread_once needs no extra link on this toolchain.

DESIGN DECISIONS

  Entry is owned, not a view. The C table lives for the life of the process, so
  borrowing would be sound, but copying the strings keeps Entry free of raw
  pointers, so it can be held across an await and moved between tasks. Two
  String allocations per lookup against a 13ms budget is not worth optimising.

  Request is consumed by reply(). Answering twice is not expressible in the
  type system rather than being rejected at runtime, and dropping a Request
  without replying gives noSuchObject - the same thing the C dispatcher does
  when a handler returns without delivering a value.

  Session is neither Send nor Sync. Every net-snmp call must stay on one thread
  (D5 / section 5.3); holding Box<dyn FnMut> makes that a compile error rather
  than a comment.

  The trampoline catches every panic. Unwinding across extern "C" is undefined
  behaviour. A panicking handler is reported as "no value", which the C side
  turns into noSuchObject.

  Two boxes for the handler, not one. The inner Box erases F; the outer one
  gives a stable thin address to hand to C as ctx. Session owns it, and both
  clear_get_handler() and Drop unregister the callback before releasing it, so
  the C side can never call through a freed pointer.

  MAX_FDS = 32 for select_info. net-snmp watches the AgentX socket and little
  else; the C side returns -1 rather than truncating if that is ever too small,
  so a wrong guess is loud rather than silent.

CLIPPY NOTE

  `u64::from(v)` on the c_ulong -> u64 conversion is a genuine widening on the
  target and a no-op on the host, so host clippy flags it and target clippy
  does not. It carries a scoped #[allow(clippy::useless_conversion)] with that
  reason, because the project's gate runs against the target.

